GROEBNER_BASES (RDG_MAPPING)
===========================================
Types, Variables, Fundamentals

[0] Ambient Algebraic Types
---------------------------
Ring R        : k[x1, ..., xn]
Ideal I       : subset of R
MonomialOrder : total well-order on monomials (lex, grevlex, etc.)
Monomial      : x1^a1 * ... * xn^an
Polynomial    : finite k-linear combination of monomials

LeadingTerm   : LT : Polynomial -> Monomial
LeadingCoeff  : LC : Polynomial -> k
LeadingMono   : LM : Polynomial -> Monomial

ClosureOp     : C_LT(I) = < LT(f) : f in I >   (monomial ideal)

GroebnerBasis : G = {g1, ..., gs} subset of I
                 satisfying < LT(G) > = C_LT(I)

SPolynomial   : S(g_i, g_j) = (lcm(LT(g_i), LT(g_j))/LT(g_i))*g_i
                             - (lcm(LT(g_i), LT(g_j))/LT(g_j))*g_j

Reduction     : f ->_G remainder r after multivariate division by G


[1] RDG Tri-Mode Types
----------------------
SID = Structure, Interaction, Dynamics

SID_Structure(S)
----------------
S.Objects:
  - R, I, monomials, polynomials
  - MonomialOrder
  - LeadingTerm map LT
  - Closure operator C_LT

S.FundamentalRelation:
  - Monomial divisibility
  - Ideal inclusion
  - Closure: C_LT(I)

S.GenerativeBasis:
  - Groebner basis G is S-minimal generative basis for C_LT(I)

SID_Interaction(I)
------------------
I.Objects:
  - Pairs (g_i, g_j)
  - Overlap monomials: lcm(LT(g_i), LT(g_j))
  - S-polynomials S(g_i, g_j)

I.Relations:
  - Reduction of S-polynomials modulo G
  - Buchberger criterion: all S(g_i, g_j) reduce to 0

I.Closure:
  - "Interaction-closure" = no unresolved overlaps
  - Confluence of reduction system

SID_Dynamics(D)
---------------
D.Process:
  - Start with generating set G0
  - Compute S-polynomials
  - Reduce S-polynomials
  - Add nonzero remainders
  - Iterate: G0 -> G1 -> ... -> G∞

D.FixedPoint:
  - G∞ = Groebner basis
  - All interactions resolved
  - Reduction yields canonical normal form

D.NormalForm:
  - f ↦ remainder(f, G∞)


[2] RDG Projection / Elimination
--------------------------------
VariableFiltration:
  - Lex order x1 > x2 > ... > xn induces:
      R ⊇ k[x2,...,xn] ⊇ ... ⊇ k[xn]

ProjectionFunctor:
  - π_l : R -> k[x_{l+1},...,xn]
  - Forget variables x1,...,xl

EliminationIdeal:
  - I_l = I ∩ k[x_{l+1},...,xn]

EliminationBasis:
  - G_l = G ∩ k[x_{l+1},...,xn]
  - G_l is Groebner basis for I_l

RDG_ProjectionLaw:
  - Restricting an S-minimal, I-closed basis along π_l
    preserves S-minimality and I-closure.

Geometry:
  - V(I_l) = closure of π_l(V(I))


[3] Example System in RDG Form
------------------------------
System:
  f1 = x^2 + y + z - 1
  f2 = x + y^2 + z - 1
  f3 = x + y + z^2 - 1

Lex order: x > y > z

D-Dynamics:
  - Buchberger evolution produces G containing:
      E(z) = z^2 (z-1)^2 (z^2 + 2z - 1)
    plus triangular expressions:
      y = Y(z)
      x = X(z)

S-Projection:
  - G_2 = G ∩ k[z] = { E(z) }

I-Closure:
  - No unresolved S-polynomial interactions in k[z]

Geometry:
  - Roots of E(z) give all possible z-coordinates
  - Each lifts to (x,y,z) via triangular relations


[4] Summary Mapping
-------------------
Groebner Basis ↦ S-minimal generative basis for LT-closure
S-Polynomials ↦ I-interaction objects
Buchberger Algorithm ↦ D-dynamics to fixed point
Normal Forms ↦ D-canonical states
Elimination ↦ RDG projection morphism preserving S/I-closure
